+## 9. Setting B choice (round 2)
+
+**A** (fixed by the item): `--spec-type ngram-mod` at the recon defaults
+(`--spec-ngram-mod-n-min 48`, `--spec-ngram-mod-n-max 64`,
+`--spec-ngram-mod-n-match 24` — §2). This is the ngram-mod variant probed in
+`docs/engine-review.md` (162 tok/s on repeated text; the live entry has no
+`--spec-*` flags, §1) and the only ngram variant with prior evidence.
+
+**B**: `--spec-type ngram-mod --spec-ngram-mod-n-max 128` — same ngram-mod
+mechanism as A, only the max draft length doubled (64→128); n-min and n-match
+stay at their defaults.
+
+Justification (from `spec_flags.txt`):
+- The size levers for the ngram family are
+  `--spec-ngram-mod-n-min` / `--spec-ngram-mod-n-max` / `--spec-ngram-mod-n-match`
+  (self-speculation over the prompt's own n-grams; no separate draft weights,
+  so the `--spec-draft-*` levers are inapplicable).
+- A and B share the validated mechanism, so the A→B delta isolates one lever
+  — draft length — which is what the 162 tok/s engine-review number (a single
+  default-size point) supports. Agent traffic re-emits the same suffixes
+  (tool outputs, prompt scaffolding) verbatim over many turns; the 64-token
+  ngram-mod cap is the documented default, so doubling to 128 tests whether
+  longer repeated suffixes (e.g. long tool results) can be captured at no extra
+  model load (the ngram pool is per-context, per-context state only).
+- Alternative considered: a different *type* (`ngram-simple`, `ngram-map-k`,
+  `ngram-map-k4v`, `ngram-cache`) would change the mechanism as well, so the
+  A/B delta would no longer say what the deployed ngram-mod variant should run
+  with; a second size is the more informative choice.
+- A third option (ngram-mod n-max 32 / n-min 16, smaller draft) is only useful
+  if the 64 cap is already costing overhead; A answers that first.
+
+## 10. Replay execution (round 2)
+
+| run | label | sampling | phase dir | status |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| off | `off` | greedy | `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/off-greedy/` | done 2026-10-12 (771 s, `result ok`) |
+| A | `A` | greedy | `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/A-greedy/` | pending (next probe) |
+| B | `B` | greedy | `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/B-greedy/` | pending |
+| off | `off` | prod | `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/off-prod/` | pending |
+| best | A or B | prod | `bench/results/2026-10-12/m1-ngram/replay/{best}-prod/` | pending — best = the higher agent-decode tok/s of A/B in the greedy dual phase |
+
+off-greedy summary: 30 agent fg + 12 mem fg per phase; all four phases
+present in `requests.jsonl`, `vram.txt`, and server logs; VRAM flat at
+9507/10240 MiB across phases. `requests.jsonl` rows: 151
+(single-agent: 30 agent fg; single-mem: 12 mem fg; dual-agent: 30 agent fg +
+60 mem bg; dual-mem: 12 mem fg + 7 agent bg — bg loops run until fg
+completes).
+
+Script fixes applied before the A run (advisor round, `bench/spec_replay.py`):
+(1) `draft_n`/`draft_n_accepted` default to `0` on missing, so spec configs
+have integer fields on every fg row; (2) the `-nommproj` fallback fires only
+on the specific mmproj-region refusals ("not supported by this context" =
+`server-context.cpp:1168`; "unsupported batch.has_embd" = :1520) — `--spec-type`
+configs can no longer be rerouted on unrelated failures (server crashes still
+abort the run as `result failed`); (3) abort before Popen if
+`:{PORT}/health` already answers (stale server would own the port);
+(4) per-phase `finish_reason` counts in `notes.txt` so capped (length) outputs
+are visible — off-greedy had 1 length finish: baseline-r1/2/8, 1323/1323
+tokens, single-agent.
+
+Observation (affects the "temp-0 outputs identical" question): the same
+request body produced *different* outputs on two independent servers
+(temperature 0, seed 0): baseline-r1/2/8 = 1323 tokens/`length` in
+single-agent vs 435 tokens/`tool_calls` in dual-agent, same id, max_tokens,
+prompt_n, cache_n. Shorter outputs differ too (e.g. mem r1/1/0: 370 vs 381
+predicted tokens across phases). Cross-server temp-0 determinism is **not**
+guaranteed on this GPU (matmul ordering); the identical-output criterion must
+be checked per arm via `out_sha256` with match counts reported, and treated
+as "not guaranteed" in the decision.
+
+Note: off-greedy was collected with the pre-fix script (its `draft_n`/
+`draft_n_accepted` rows are null, the documented absence for the no-spec config
+— §7). A, B, off-prod and best-prod run with the fixed script.
